\section{Quote Extraction}
\label{sec:quote-extraction}

Downloading a PDF is only the first step in verifying that a source actually
supports a claim.
The reviewer must still find the verbatim passage that backs the claim---the
\texttt{QUOTE} recorded in a citation's provenance.
This section turns a cached PDF and a claim into a short list of candidate
supporting passages, each tagged with the page it came from.

Crucially, the judgement stays with the human: we only \emph{propose} passages
and their locations.
The same machinery serves two callers---the [[scholar pdf quote]] command, and
the LLM theme classifier, which uses a paper's full text (not just its
abstract) to assign labels.
So everything here is plain library code with no CLI or console dependency.

\subsection{How the pieces fit together}

Finding candidates is a pipeline of four steps, each its own function so that
callers can recombine them:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Extract} per-page text from the PDF ([[extract_pdf_pages]]).
\item \textbf{Segment} that text into sentence-level passages
  ([[segment_passages]]).
\item \textbf{Rank} passages against the claim.  Two independent rankers are
  available: a cheap lexical overlap score ([[rank_passages_lexical]]) and an
  LLM re-ranker ([[rerank_passages_llm]]).
\item \textbf{Orchestrate} the above behind one entry point
  ([[find_quote_candidates]]), where the caller chooses lexical, LLM, or both.
\end{enumerate}

The two rankers are deliberately separable.
A human reading CLI output benefits from the LLM's semantic judgement, so the
command runs both by default.
The classifier, by contrast, already makes one LLM call per batch to assign
labels; paying for a second LLM call per paper just to order passages would be
wasteful, so it runs the lexical ranker alone and lets the classification call
do the judging.

\subsection{Passage and candidate types}

A [[Passage]] is a span of source text with the page it was found on; a
[[QuoteCandidate]] adds the scores that ranking produces.
We keep them as separate frozen types because a [[Passage]] is raw input to
ranking, whereas a [[QuoteCandidate]] is a ranked result the caller consumes.
Page numbers are 1-based throughout, matching how a reader cites \enquote{p.3}.

<<quote imports>>=
import json
import re
from dataclasses import dataclass
