\subsection{Extracting page text}

We extract text with [[pypdfium2]], the same engine the snowball submodule
uses for PDF parsing, so we add no new dependency.
The import is deferred to call time: most [[scholar]] invocations never touch a
PDF's text, and keeping the (heavy, native) binding out of module import means
quote extraction can degrade to \enquote{no quotes} on a machine where it is
missing rather than breaking the whole module.

Returning one string per page---rather than one concatenated blob---is what
lets us report the page a passage came from for free: the list index \emph{is}
the page, minus one.

<<quote extraction functions>>=
def extract_pdf_pages(path: Path) -> list[str]:
    """Return the text of each page of a PDF, one string per page.

    The list is 0-indexed; entry ``n`` is printed page ``n + 1``.

    Raises:
        ImportError: If pypdfium2 is not installed.
    """
    import pypdfium2 as pdfium

    pages: list[str] = []
    document = pdfium.PdfDocument(str(path))
    try:
        for index in range(len(document)):
            page = document[index]
            textpage = page.get_textpage()
            pages.append(textpage.get_text_range())
            textpage.close()
            page.close()
    finally:
        document.close()
    return pages
@

\subsection{Segmenting passages}

PDF text extraction preserves the visual layout: lines are broken where they
wrapped on the page, and words are hyphenated across line breaks.
Left as-is, this defeats both token matching (\enquote{authen-ticated} matches
neither \enquote{authenticated} nor the claim) and the goal of a clean verbatim
quote.
We therefore reflow each page before splitting it, undoing line-break
hyphenation first and then treating remaining newlines as soft wraps.

Sentence-level granularity is the right unit for a supporting quote: it is long
enough to be self-contained evidence yet short enough to cite directly.
We drop passages below [[MIN_PASSAGE_CHARS]] because short fragments---page
numbers, running heads, table cells---are noise, not quotable support.

<<quote extraction constants>>=
# Passages shorter than this are usually fragments (page numbers, headers,
# table cells) that make poor verbatim quotes.
MIN_PASSAGE_CHARS = 40
@

<<quote extraction functions>>=
def segment_passages(pages: list[str]) -> list[Passage]:
    """Split per-page text into sentence-level passages.

    Line wrapping and end-of-line hyphenation from PDF layout are undone
    first, so each passage reads as running prose. Fragments shorter than
    ``MIN_PASSAGE_CHARS`` are dropped.

    Args:
        pages: Per-page text as returned by ``extract_pdf_pages``.

    Returns:
        Passages in reading order, each tagged with its 1-based page.
    """
    passages: list[Passage] = []
    for page_index, page_text in enumerate(pages):
        for sentence in _split_sentences(_reflow_text(page_text)):
            sentence = sentence.strip()
            if len(sentence) >= MIN_PASSAGE_CHARS:
                passages.append(Passage(page=page_index + 1, text=sentence))
    return passages
@

The reflow and sentence-splitting helpers are kept private: they encode
heuristics specific to extracted PDF text and are not useful on their own.

<<quote extraction constants>>=
_SENTENCE_BOUNDARY = re.compile(r"(?<=[.!?])\s+")
@

<<quote extraction functions>>=
def _reflow_text(text: str) -> str:
    """Undo PDF line-wrapping: de-hyphenate, then collapse whitespace."""
    # pypdfium2 marks a soft-hyphen line break it has already joined with
    # U+FFFE; dropping it (and any literal soft hyphen) restores
    # "optimiza<FFFE>tion" -> "optimization".
    text = text.replace("\ufffe", "").replace("\u00ad", "")
    # "authen-\nticated" -> "authenticated"
    text = re.sub(r"-\n\s*", "", text)
    # Remaining newlines are soft wraps within a paragraph.
    text = re.sub(r"\s*\n\s*", " ", text)
    return re.sub(r"\s{2,}", " ", text).strip()


def _split_sentences(text: str) -> list[str]:
    """Split running text into sentences on terminal punctuation."""
    return _SENTENCE_BOUNDARY.split(text) if text else []
@

