        passages = [
            Passage(
                page=2,
                text="The weather in spring is mild and pleasant across "
                "the northern coastal regions this year.",
            ),
            Passage(
                page=3,
                text="An authenticated encryption scheme provides both "
                "confidentiality and integrity.",
            ),
        ]
        ranked = rank_passages_lexical(
            "authenticated encryption provides confidentiality and integrity",
            passages,
            limit=5,
        )
        assert ranked[0].page == 3

    def test_empty_claim_returns_nothing(self):
        passages = [
            Passage(page=1, text="A sufficiently long passage of text here.")
        ]
        assert rank_passages_lexical("the a of to", passages, limit=5) == []
@

\subsection{LLM re-ranking}

The lexical score cannot tell whether a passage \emph{asserts} the claim or
merely shares its vocabulary.
When semantic judgement matters, we hand the candidates to an LLM and ask it to
order and label them.

Two safeguards keep this honest.
First, the model sees the passages but never supplies their text: we look the
text back up from our own candidates by index, so quotes remain verbatim.
Second, we distinguish failure from rejection.
A genuine failure---no model configured, a network error, or output with no
parseable JSON array---returns [[None]], so the orchestrator falls back to the
prior order and warns.
A model that ran and returned an \emph{empty} array has instead judged nothing
relevant; we return an empty list and honour that, rather than misreporting it
as a failure and showing passages the model rejected.
We follow the [[llm]] usage pattern already established in [[llm_review]]:
[[llm.get_model(model_id)]] when a model is named, else the user's default.

<<quote extraction functions>>=
def rerank_passages_llm(
    claim: str,
    candidates: list[QuoteCandidate],
    model_id: str | None = None,
) -> list[QuoteCandidate] | None:
    """Re-rank candidate passages for relevance to the claim using an LLM.

    The model receives the claim and the numbered candidates and returns a
    JSON array of ``{"index": i, "relevance": "high|medium|low"}`` ordered
    best first. Passage text is taken from ``candidates``, never the model,
    so quotes stay verbatim.

    Returns:
        Re-ranked candidates with ``relevance`` set; an empty list if the
        model ran but judged none of the candidates relevant; or ``None`` if
        no model is available or the response could not be parsed (so the
        caller can fall back to the prior order).
    """
    if not candidates:
        return []
    try:
        import llm

        model = llm.get_model(model_id) if model_id else llm.get_model()
        prompt = _build_rerank_prompt(claim, candidates)
        ranking = _parse_rerank_response(model.prompt(prompt).text())
    except Exception as error:  # treat any error as a failure to rank
        logger.debug("LLM rerank failed, falling back: %s", error)
        return None

    if ranking is None:
        logger.debug("LLM rerank returned no usable JSON; falling back")
        return None

    reranked: list[QuoteCandidate] = []
    seen: set[int] = set()
    for index, relevance in ranking:
        if 0 <= index < len(candidates) and index not in seen:
            seen.add(index)
            base = candidates[index]
            reranked.append(
                QuoteCandidate(
                    page=base.page,
                    text=base.text,
                    score=base.score,
                    relevance=relevance,
                )
            )
    return reranked
@

The prompt and parser are private helpers.
The parser is lenient about \emph{entries}---it skips a malformed item rather
than discarding an otherwise usable ranking---but strict about
\emph{structure}: if it finds no JSON array, or the array does not decode, it
returns [[None]] so the caller can tell a parse failure apart from a
legitimately empty result.

<<quote extraction functions>>=
def _build_rerank_prompt(
    claim: str,
    candidates: list[QuoteCandidate],
) -> str:
    """Build the LLM prompt asking it to rank candidates by relevance."""
    lines = [
        "You are helping verify whether a source supports a claim.",
        "",
        f"Claim: {claim}",
        "",
        "Candidate passages (verbatim from the source):",
    ]
    for index, candidate in enumerate(candidates):
        lines.append(f"[{index}] (p.{candidate.page}) {candidate.text}")
    lines.append("")
    lines.append(
        "Return ONLY a JSON array of the passages that could support the "
        "claim, most relevant first, each as "
        '{"index": <int>, "relevance": "high|medium|low"}. '
        "Omit passages that are irrelevant."
    )
    return "\n".join(lines)


def _parse_rerank_response(
    response_text: str,
) -> list[tuple[int, str | None]] | None:
    """Parse the LLM's JSON ranking into (index, relevance) pairs.

    Returns ``None`` when no JSON array can be found or decoded---a failure
    the caller treats as "no ranking". Returns an empty list when the model
    returned a valid but empty array (it judged nothing relevant).
    """
    match = re.search(r"\[.*\]", response_text, re.DOTALL)
    if not match:
        return None
    try:
        data = json.loads(match.group(0))
    except json.JSONDecodeError:
        return None
    if not isinstance(data, list):
        return None
    ranking: list[tuple[int, str | None]] = []
    for item in data:
        try:
            index = int(item["index"])
        except (KeyError, TypeError, ValueError):
            continue
        relevance = str(item.get("relevance", "")).lower() or None
        ranking.append((index, relevance))
    return ranking
@

Let's verify the failure/rejection distinction: a model that returns an empty
array yields an empty list (nothing relevant), unparseable output yields
[[None]] so the orchestrator can fall back, and a valid ranking sets the order
and relevance.  We monkeypatch [[llm.get_model]] so no real model is called.

<<test functions>>=
class TestRerankPassages:
    """Tests for the LLM re-ranking stage."""

    def _patch_model(self, monkeypatch, reply):
        import llm

        model = Mock()
        model.prompt.return_value.text.return_value = reply
        monkeypatch.setattr(llm, "get_model", lambda *a, **k: model)

    def test_empty_array_means_nothing_relevant(self, monkeypatch):
        self._patch_model(monkeypatch, "[]")
        candidates = [QuoteCandidate(page=1, text="A passage of text here.")]
        assert rerank_passages_llm("claim", candidates) == []

    def test_unparseable_output_falls_back(self, monkeypatch):
        self._patch_model(monkeypatch, "I cannot answer that.")
        candidates = [QuoteCandidate(page=1, text="A passage of text here.")]
        assert rerank_passages_llm("claim", candidates) is None

    def test_ranking_sets_relevance_and_order(self, monkeypatch):
        self._patch_model(
            monkeypatch,
            '[{"index": 1, "relevance": "high"}, '
            '{"index": 0, "relevance": "low"}]',
        )
        candidates = [
            QuoteCandidate(page=1, text="First passage of text here."),
            QuoteCandidate(page=2, text="Second passage of text here."),
        ]
        ranked = rerank_passages_llm("claim", candidates)
        assert [c.page for c in ranked] == [2, 1]
        assert ranked[0].relevance == "high"
@

\subsection{Finding quote candidates}

[[find_quote_candidates]] is the single entry point both callers use.
It accepts anything [[get_pdf]] accepts---a URL, a DOI, or a [[Paper]]
object---so the CLI can pass a string and the classifier can pass the paper it
already holds.

The two ranking stages compose three ways.
With both enabled (the default), the lexical ranker prefilters to [[prefilter]]
candidates and the LLM re-ranks them---the lexical pass bounds how much the LLM
must read.
Lexical-only skips the LLM.
LLM-only has no cheap prefilter, so we cap the passages handed to the model at
[[max_llm_passages]] to bound cost, and announce the cap rather than silently
truncating.

Failure to obtain or parse the PDF returns an empty list, not an exception:
a single paywalled or unreachable paper must not abort a classification batch.
This mirrors the silent-degradation convention the search providers already
follow.

<<quote extraction functions>>=
def find_quote_candidates(
    source: Any,
    claim: str,
    *,
    limit: int = 5,
    prefilter: int = 20,
    model_id: str | None = None,
    use_lexical: bool = True,
    use_llm: bool = True,
    max_llm_passages: int = 100,
    progress_callback: Callable[[str], None] | None = None,
) -> list[QuoteCandidate]:
    """Find passages in a source that may support a claim.

    The ranking stages are independent: lexical only, LLM only, or both
    (lexical prefilter then LLM rerank). Verification stays human---this
    only proposes passages and their pages.

    Args:
        source: A Paper object, DOI, or URL accepted by ``get_pdf``.
        claim: The claim to find support for.
        limit: Maximum candidates to return.
        prefilter: Lexical shortlist size before LLM rerank (both stages).
        model_id: ``llm`` model id for reranking (llm default if None).
        use_lexical: Run the lexical ranking stage.
        use_llm: Run the LLM rerank stage.
        max_llm_passages: Cap on passages sent to the LLM in LLM-only mode.
        progress_callback: Optional callback for status messages.

    Returns:
        Up to ``limit`` candidates, best first. Empty if the PDF cannot be
        fetched or parsed, or if nothing matches.

    Raises:
        ValueError: If both ranking stages are disabled.
    """
    if not use_lexical and not use_llm:
        raise ValueError(
            "At least one of lexical or LLM ranking must be enabled"
        )

    try:
        path = get_pdf(source, progress_callback)
        passages = segment_passages(extract_pdf_pages(path))
    except PDFDownloadError as error:
        logger.info("No PDF available for quote extraction: %s", error)
        return []
    except ImportError:
        logger.warning(
            "pypdfium2 is required for quote extraction; returning no quotes"
        )
        return []

    if not passages:
        return []

    <<select candidate passages>>
    <<optionally rerank with the LLM>>
    return candidates[:limit]
@

When the lexical stage is on, it does double duty: as a prefilter (shortlist of
[[prefilter]]) when the LLM follows, or as the final ranker (top [[limit]]) when
it does not.
When it is off, we take passages in reading order up to the LLM cap.

<<select candidate passages>>=
if use_lexical:
    shortlist = prefilter if use_llm else limit
    candidates = rank_passages_lexical(claim, passages, shortlist)
else:
    candidates = [
        QuoteCandidate(page=p.page, text=p.text)
        for p in passages[:max_llm_passages]
    ]
    if len(passages) > max_llm_passages and progress_callback:
        progress_callback(
            f"Considering first {max_llm_passages} of "
            f"{len(passages)} passages for LLM ranking"
        )
@

If the rerank returns a list---even an empty one---we adopt it: an empty list
is the model saying nothing is relevant, which we honour.
Only [[None]] (a failure) makes us keep the candidates we already have (lexical
order, or reading order in LLM-only mode) and warn.

<<optionally rerank with the LLM>>=
if use_llm:
    reranked = rerank_passages_llm(claim, candidates, model_id)
    if reranked is None:
        if progress_callback:
            progress_callback("LLM rerank unavailable; keeping prior order")
    else:
        candidates = reranked
@

Finally, let's verify the orchestrator: it refuses to run with no ranker,
finds the supporting passage in lexical-only mode, and degrades to an empty
list when the PDF cannot be fetched.  We monkeypatch the PDF-access functions
so no network or real PDF is needed.

<<test functions>>=
class TestFindQuoteCandidates:
    """Tests for the quote-extraction orchestrator."""

    def test_requires_a_ranking_stage(self):
        with pytest.raises(ValueError):
            find_quote_candidates(
                "https://example.com/p.pdf",
                "claim",
                use_lexical=False,
                use_llm=False,
            )

    def test_lexical_only_finds_supporting_passage(
